ACTION: REVISE
TITLE: Unified Video Tokenizers for Improved Discrete Autoregressive Video Generation
 REVISED Proposal from 1: Instead of fine-tuning a pre-trained discrete image tokenizer directly on a video dataset, leverage contrastive learning to adapt the tokenizer to video reconstruction tasks. This involves contrasting video-token sequences with;


a) video representations obtained from state-of-the-art video diffusion models (e.g., DM-VNV); 
b) a dummy sequence of the same token counts and spatial dimensions; 
c) a sequence of shuffled tokens.

We evaluate the adaptability of the introduced adaptive learning approach on Vimeo-90K and its robustness against large-scale variations in video lengths and token counts.
CONTROLS: Comparing with state-of-the-art tokenizers trained from scratch or initialized from pre-trained image tokenizers.
COST: 80 GPU-hours
PREDICTION: The contrastive learning approach will prove effective in adaptating the tokenizer, yielding better video reconstruction quality while avoiding some of the pre-training drawbacks, and its recognition and generalization capabilities might outperform fine-tuned tokenizers trained from scratch.
